\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The kept form is a Schur complement}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Let the edge weights be strictly positive and the graph
connected, and let #{K} be a nonempty set of vertices. Write
#{V} for the vertex set and let
#{h} be the counting inner product on #{\mathbb R^V}, and
let #{h_K} be the counting inner product on #{\mathbb R^K}.
Split
#{\mathbb R^V=\mathbb R^K\oplus\mathbb R^{V\setminus K}}
orthogonally, and let #{i_K}, #{i_U}, #{p_K} and #{p_U} be the
inclusions and orthogonal projections of the two summands.
The weighted Laplacian #{L_w} is self-adjoint for #{h}. Set
#{A=p_K L_w i_K}, #{C=p_U L_w i_U} and #{B=p_K L_w i_U}, and
let #{B^*} be the counting adjoint of #{B}, so
#{B^*=p_U L_w i_K}. If #{K} omits at least one vertex, then
#{C} is positive definite, and the \vocabk{kept form}{fdip-00DF}
is #{h_K(u,Su)}, where
##{S=A-BC^{-1}B^*}
is the \vocabk{Schur complement}{fdip-000D} of #{C}. The
minimizer on the eliminated summand is #{-C^{-1}B^*u}, and it
is the unique extension whose Laplacian vanishes off #{K}.
When #{K=V} there is no eliminated summand and the kept form
is the energy. For distinct vertices #{a} and #{b}, the kept
form on the vertex set #{\{a,b\}} of the function that equals
#{1} at #{a} and #{0} at #{b} equals the
\vocabk{effective conductance}{fdip-00DF} #{c(a,b)}.}

\proof{
\p{The case in which #{K} is the whole vertex set is the energy
itself. Suppose some vertex lies outside #{K}. Declare the
counting basis of vertex indicators with the vertices of #{K}
written first. In that basis
##{L_w=\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\ B^T&C\end{pmatrix},}
where these blocks are the matrices of the operators #{A},
#{B}, #{C} and #{B^*} in the statement, and #{B^T} is the
matrix of #{B^*}. If #{y^T C y=0}, extend #{y} by
#{0} on #{K}. The energy of the extension is a sum of
strictly positive weights times squared edge differences, so
every edge difference vanishes. The graph is connected, and
the extension vanishes on #{K}, so the extension is #{0} and
#{y=0}. Thus #{C} is positive definite.}

\p{For an extension #{(u,y)}, the energy expands as
#{u^T A u+2u^T B y+y^T C y}. Set #{K_\ast=C^{-1}B^T}. Then
##{y^T C y+2u^T B y
=(y+K_\ast u)^T C(y+K_\ast u)
-u^T BC^{-1}B^T u.}
The completed square is nonnegative and vanishes at
#{y=-K_\ast u}. The minimum value is #{u^T S u}. In this
basis that value is #{h_K(u,Su)}, and #{y=-K_\ast u} is the
coordinate vector of #{-C^{-1}B^*u}. At that
extension the block equation #{C y+B^T u=0} says that the
Laplacian vanishes off #{K}. Positive definiteness makes the
extension unique. The same completion is written in the proof of
\ref{fdip-000D}.}

\p{Now let #{K=\{a,b\}}, let #{u} equal #{1} at #{a} and #{0}
at #{b}, and let #{v} be its minimizing extension. Then
#{L_w v} vanishes off #{\{a,b\}}. Each generator of #{L_w} is orthogonal to constant
fields, so the two remaining values sum to #{0}:
#{L_w v=(L_w v)(a)\,(\delta_a-\delta_b)}. The energy is #{v^T L_w v}. It receives a contribution only
from #{a} and #{b}. Since #{v(a)=1} and #{v(b)=0}, the energy
equals #{\alpha=(L_w v)(a)}. The extension #{v} is not
constant, so #{\alpha>0}. Subtract a constant
from #{v/\alpha} so that the result #{\phi} is orthogonal to
constant fields. Then #{L_w\phi=\delta_a-\delta_b}, and
\ref{fdip-005U} gives #{\phi=L_w^+(\delta_a-\delta_b)}. Hence
##{(\delta_a-\delta_b)^T L_w^+(\delta_a-\delta_b)
=\phi(a)-\phi(b)=\frac{1}{\alpha}.}
In this counting basis that product is the pairing
#{h(\delta_a-\delta_b,L_w^+(\delta_a-\delta_b))}.
The reciprocal is the energy of #{v}, which is the kept form
of #{u}.}
}
